    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Though sterol biosynthesis and function are well understood in eukaryotes, the role of sterols in prokaryotes is widely unknown. Using bioinformatics, novel bacterial sterol transport (Bst) proteins were identified, used in binding studies, and structurally characterized to support the discovery of a novel tripartite sterol transport system in Methylococcus capsulatus. Further bioinformatics and protein homology comparisons revealed two proteins of interest in Enhygromyxa salina, a novel cholesterol producing bacterium. Both were produced recombinantly and structurally characterized. This thesis attempts to identify substrates for these proteins. It was found that Es_TatT binds to long-chain fatty acids than a sterol metabolite, while a gas chromatography/mass spectrometry workflow was developed in attempt to identify the ligand of Es_BstC. These results mark the exploration of poorly characterized prokaryotic protein families while also demonstrating the development of robust experimental methods to study these proteins.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Oo, H. and Dassama, L. (2025). Characterization of Bacterial Sterol Transporter (Bst) Proteins and Development of a Generalizable Workflow for Ligand Identification. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/tp055mg6994. https://doi.org/10.25740/tp055mg6994.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
